# FutureVera – Notes (TryHackMe)

This one looked simple at first but ended up being a good reminder to **trust the challenge description**.

Started with a standard scan:

```bash
nmap -sC -sV -T4 <IP>
```

Nothing unusual — SSH and a web server. Mapped the domain in `/etc/hosts`:

```text
10.146.157.118 futurevera.thm
```

Did some quick directory enumeration:

```bash
gobuster dir -u https://futurevera.thm -w /usr/share/wordlists/dirb/common.txt -k
```

Found `/assets`, `/css`, `/js`. Looked into them, especially the JS:

```bash
curl -k https://futurevera.thm/js/scripts.js
```

…but nothing useful. Static template stuff.

At that point I was basically stuck, and that’s when I realized I ignored the biggest hint —
the challenge literally says it’s about **subdomain enumeration**.

Switched approach:

```bash
gobuster vhost -u https://futurevera.thm \
-w /usr/share/seclists/Discovery/DNS/subdomains-top1million-5000.txt \
--append-domain -k
```

Found:

* `blog.futurevera.thm`
* `support.futurevera.thm`

Added them to `/etc/hosts` and moved on.

The support subdomain didn’t give much. No inputs, no obvious attack surface, nothing to exploit directly. That’s usually a sign you need to go deeper instead of wider.

Next step was checking the certificate:

```bash
openssl s_client -connect support.futurevera.thm:443 \
-servername support.futurevera.thm 2>/dev/null \
| openssl x509 -noout -text
```

That revealed a new subdomain inside the cert:

```
secrethelpdesk934752.support.futurevera.thm
```

Added it to `/etc/hosts` and accessed it. Same situation again — normal page, nothing obvious. At this point it felt like a dead end.

Eventually checked the headers instead of the page content:

```bash
curl --head https://secrethelpdesk934752.support.futurevera.thm
```

That’s where the flag was hiding — inside a redirect:

```
Location: http://flag{beea0d6edfcee06a59b83fb50ae81b2f}...
```

---

## Takeaways

* Read the challenge description carefully — it usually tells you the path
* Subdomain enumeration isn’t just brute force → certificates matter
* When pages look “too clean”, check:

  * headers
  * redirects
  * metadata
* Don’t get stuck on directories if the problem is elsewhere

